Z||000009||Phaeton||12-04-1999||08:35 PM||||I gather the problem started when you put in the new ECM?  Did you use the old PROM or a new one?  Was the PROM put in backwards (they swing both ways)?  Blue wire to injector (2.5) should not be hot, tracing shorts is not fun...but its getting power from somewhere it shouldn't and is the most likely scenario.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Mark||12-04-1999||08:55 PM||||Bj, it's normal to measure 12 VDC at both sides of the injector when the engine is not running.  One side of the injector is connected directly to a 12 VDC source (via the ECM fuse).  Current flows through the injector, and returns to ground through the ECM.  If the engine is not running, the side of the injector connected to the ECM "floats" at 12 VDC.  The Service Engine Soon light operates the same way (i.e., the ECM provides the current return path to ground).  <BR>Your ECM may not be running.  Make certain your ECM is properly grounded. There appears to be more than one ground connection to the ECM (per the Haynes manual).||||reg||1
